// @emit-error emitProgram: an f32[] return must be annotated (`: f32[]`)
// A NAMED function with an UN-ANNOTATED return whose body returns an `f32[]` binding is
// an EMIT REJECT, while its `f64[]` and `i64[]` siblings — the same three-line shape with
// only the leaf type changed — both run:
//
//     function mk() { const xs: f64[] = [1.5]; return xs }   // prints 1.5
//     function mk() { const xs: i64[] = [6e9]; return xs }   // prints 6000000000
//     function mk() { const xs: f32[] = [1.5]; return xs }   // THIS FILE — rejects
//
// Found by GRADING A ZERO, not by a bug report. `emit_collect`'s A20 inferred-return
// chain has an `f32[]` arm (`fRetKind[li] = "f32list"`) that is reached only when the
// function has NO return annotation, and a sabotage that answers that arm WRONG is inert
// on every channel this compiler has: 1,487 corpus files (wasm sha256 + build status +
// diagnostics), 25,200 fuzz programs, and 24 hand-constructed candidate shapes. The
// comparator was live throughout — the same sweep fires on `valueAtomKind`'s and the
// `nulstr` arm's sabotages.
//
// This file is WHY the arm has no witness: its intended input never gets that far. The
// checker DOES record the name (`isScalarListRetName` admits `i64[]`/`f32[]`/`f64[]`,
// which is why that classifier's `f32` arm is live while its `f64` arm is not), but the
// f32 list's wrapper/backing types are never forced for this binding. So the A20 f32 arm
// is unreachable BEHIND a defect, not merely untested — the distinction a coverage zero
// alone cannot make.
//
// THE MESSAGE MOVED ONE FUNCTION EARLIER, and the reject it replaces was a symptom.
// This file used to pin `emitProgram: index access but array type not collected` — raised
// at the CALLER's `mk()[0]`, because the read could not find the list types. That reject
// hid the real state of the callee: with any `f32[]` ANNOTATION elsewhere in the program
// the collection succeeded, the caller-side reject never fired, and `mk` itself emitted
// INVALID WASM (its functype result is the `"i32"` default while its body returns the
// f32 list's `(ref $array)`) — with `vl check` rc 0, and on a `mk` that need not even be
// CALLED. `emitReturnValue` now names the disagreement at the return itself, so all nine
// spellings in the family — annotated / `let` / local binding, call argument, UNREAD
// binding, never-called callee, `mk()[0]`, `mk().length`, bare binding — draw one loud
// error instead of six invalid modules and three symptoms.
//
// The remedy the message names is real and is pinned beside this file: the ANNOTATED
// return (`f32-array-return.vl`) lowers correctly. So does a returned f32-list PARAM
// (`function mk(a: f32[]) { return a }`) — `criClassify` CAN classify a param, so its
// stored kind is `"f32list"` and the guard, keyed on the `"i32"` default rather than on
// the return expression alone, does not fire.
//
// When an inferred-return f32-list binding gets its wrapper/backing types forced —
// `emit_collect`'s A20 arm plus `criClassify`'s returned-LOCAL classification, neither
// reachable from `wasmEmit.vl` — this graduates to `@run` (expected `1.5`) and the A20
// `f32[]` arm gets its first pin.
function mk() {
  const xs: f32[] = [1.5]
  return xs
}
function main() {
  print(mk()[0])
}
main()
